 ### [CUDA Volume Ray Casting](2048-cuda-volume-ray-casting.md)
-CUDA Volume Ray Casting is a direct volume rendering method that parallelizes the ray casting algorithm on the NVIDIA GPU using the CUDA programming model, avoiding intermediate geometric entities to directly generate 2D images from 3D datasets. It differs from shader-based GPU ray casting by utilizing explicit CUDA memory management (CUDA arrays and surfaces) for zero-overhead interoperability with OpenGL, allowing the GPU to write directly to output buffers without intermediate CPU round-trips. It is chosen when fine-grained control over memory access patterns and kernel execution is required compared to fixed-function or shader-based pipelines.
+CUDA Volume Ray Casting is a direct volume rendering method that parallelizes the ray casting algorithm on the NVIDIA GPU using the CUDA programming model, avoiding intermediate geometric entities to directly generate 2D images from 3D datasets. It assigns one thread per pixel to cast rays through a 3D volume texture, sampling scalar values, mapping them to color and opacity via a transfer function, and compositing the result using front-to-back alpha blending with early termination. It sets itself apart from standard OpenGL shader-based approaches by providing explicit control over memory hierarchy (textures, surfaces, and global memory) and kernel launch parameters, often yielding performance improvements over generic GPU-based implementations.
